            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Perseverance rover news is that NASA says the rover has been investigating the “Broom Point member,” a roughly 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient layered rock on the rim of Jezero Crater. Scientists interpret the rocks as a record built by repeated asteroid impacts, and NASA says the layered bedrock may be more than 3.9 billion years old【6:1†source】【6:2†source】.\n\nA related update is that Perseverance captured a sweeping Mastcam-Z view looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater’s rim from Broom Point, helping scientists study the terrain and plan future investigations【6:1†source】【6:2†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the Martian equivalent of a marathon—26.2 miles / 42.195 km—reaching that distance on its 1,890th Martian day, or sol. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter imaged the rover and its tracks from orbit around the time it crossed the milestone【6:0†source】.",
